coi jbopre,=20

The selma'o CUhE contains two cmavo, namely the eponymous {cu'e} and also {=
nau}. (Four if you count the experimentals {pu'au} and {ba'au}.)

The CLL mentions CUhE in two places -- section 10.24, which talks about {cu=
'e} but not {nau}, and in the catalog of selma'o. The omission of {nau} in =
10.24 is too bad, but if we demanded the CLL cover all cmavo it would be to=
o heavy to ship by Amazon drone. However, the catalog states that CUhE is f=
or asking tense/modal questions. That's the function of {cu'e}, but not {na=
u} (nor {pu'au}/{ba'au}, for that matter). Shouldn't the catalog say that C=
UhE is for "combined space/time tenses", or something to that effect?

I see from Nick Nicholas' note in jbovlaste that the current meaning of {na=
u} came about in 1991, before which I suppose it was in LAU, making CUhE a =
singleton selma'o, so perhaps that explains the selma'o catalog entry.

I apologize in advance if this has been discussed before. I didn't see it i=
n the CLL Errata page, and I searched the list for mention of CUhE and all =
I found was the invention of {re'u} (which was pretty neat to see).

mu'o mi'e la durka

